# 3. Running ROS 2 tools inside Docker

Open a Docker terminal:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it diffbot_jazzy bash
```

### RViz2

```bash
rviz2
```

Save your RViz configuration inside `/data` so it persists:

```bash
rviz2 -d /data/diffbot.rviz
```

The second command works after you have saved a configuration to that path.

### RQT Graph

```bash
ros2 run rqt_graph rqt_graph
```

This visualizes communication between ROS nodes.

### RQT GUI

```bash
rqt
```

### TF tree visualization

```bash
ros2 run rqt_tf_tree rqt_tf_tree
```

### Generate TF frame diagram

```bash
cd /data
ros2 run tf2_tools view_frames
```

This generates a TF diagram you can inspect.

### Inspect a specific transform

```bash
ros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo odom base_footprint
```

### Check ROS 2 communication

```bash
ros2 node list
ros2 topic list
ros2 service list
ros2 action list
```

### Check DiffBot topics

```bash
ros2 topic echo /joint_states --once
ros2 topic echo /odom --once
ros2 topic hz /scan
ros2 topic info /scan -v
```

### Check ROS 2 Control

```bash
ros2 control list_controllers
ros2 control list_hardware_interfaces
```

These commands query the controller manager running on your Raspberry Pi, provided ROS 2 networking is working.

